# Vendored fram validation sources.
#
# The native-core validation drivers project real fram modules through the
# native pipeline and byte-compare the result against a committed projection.
# Reading those modules out of a live ~/code/fram checkout made every beagle
# gate a function of another repository's working tree: an unrelated fram
# commit turned a beagle driver red, and — because bin/_gate-cache-run keys a
# gate on the whole traced input closure — moved the cache key too. Copies
# under this directory make the traced closure beagle-only, and a fram change
# arrives as an explicit bump commit that carries the source diff next to the
# projection diff.
#
# A revision pin plus `git -C ~/code/fram/main show <rev>:path` does NOT work
# here: reading through fram's .git sweeps pack files into the traced closure,
# so the key would bust on every fram commit while the projection stayed put.
#
# License: fram is dual MIT/Apache (LICENSE-MIT, LICENSE-APACHE in that repo),
# the same terms this repository carries; vendoring is a layout decision.
#
# Bumping: copy direct files from the fram checkout; for a derived source,
# generate its canonical labeled patch with
# `LC_ALL=C diff -u --label upstream --label derived INPUT OUTPUT`, name the
# patch by its SHA-256, and prove strict reverse/forward replay. Update
# `revision` and all digests, re-run the affected drivers, and commit the
# regenerated projections in the SAME commit.
# native-core/tests/fram_upstream_freshness.sh reports drift against a live
# checkout and never fails a gate.
#
# The set is the drivers' transitive read closure, not just the modules they
# name: `beagle-ast` resolves a `(require fram.slots …)` by opening the sibling
# source, so src/fram/slots.bgl is here even though no driver mentions it. An
# omission shows up as a projection that no longer matches — the AST loses the
# externs the missing module supplied.
#
# Direct format: `<sha256>  <path-within-fram>`.
# Derived format: `derived-patch-v1 <vendored-sha256> <vendored-path>
#                 <upstream-sha256> <upstream-path> <patch-sha256>`.
# Direct paths are relative to fram's root and to this directory alike. A
# derived path names the Beagle-owned result separately from its real upstream
# path so provenance never claims that a migrated file existed in Fram. Its
# patch lives at `transforms/<patch-sha256>.patch`; v1 applies with zero fuzz.

